  "worked_solution": "The first four terms of the series are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =1 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =2x \\\\ {a}_{2} & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =4{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can express these terms using a common format, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & ={x}^{0} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =(1+1){x}^{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} & =(2+1){x}^{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =(3+1){x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe deduce that the general formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=(n+1){x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+1){x}^{n}\\,.\n$$",
